<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Fountain of Bakhchisaray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fountain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai">Bakhchisaray</a> is a narrative poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Alexander Pushkin</a>, written in 1821–1823 and published in 1824. Inspired by his visit to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai-palace">Khan's Palace</a> at Bakhchysarai and its fountain, it tells of the khan Giray, his jealous wife Zarema and the captive Polish princess Maria. It was hugely popular and inspired a ballet by Boris Asafyev in 1934.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Pushkin</a> began the poem in the spring of 1821, after visiting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchisaray-fountain">Fountain of Tears</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhchysarai-palace">Bakhchysarai Palace</a> in 1820. The bulk of it was written during 1822, the draft was finished in spring 1823, and in the autumn of 1823 he made his final changes and prepared it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>. The first edition appeared on 10 March 1824. The poem has 578 lines of freely rhymed iambic tetrameter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>In the harem of the Crimean khan Giray, the Georgian concubine Zarema is sad because the khan has fallen out of love with her. His mind is on Maria, a Polish nobleman's daughter kidnapped during a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> raid. Maria is inconsolable, spends her time in prayer to the Virgin Mary and prefers death to the fate of a non-believer's concubine. At night Zarema goes to Maria's room and tries with threats to make her reject the khan, so that he will love Zarema again. Soon after, Maria dies, and the guards of the harem drown Zarema.</p><p>The khan goes to war to forget his troubles but remains tormented. On his return he orders a fountain built in the palace in memory of Maria, which the young maidens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, learning of the sad legend, call the fountain of tears. In an epilogue the narrator wanders through the abandoned palace, sees a woman's ghost and wonders whether it is Maria's or Zarema's, recalls a love for whom he yearns, and wishes to return to Crimea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Pushkin</a> scholar Sergei Bondi writes that the poem most closely resembles the canon of Romantic poems in its fragmentary form, its deliberate incoherence and ambiguity, as in the fate of Zarema and Maria, its lyricism and the musicality of its verse. He thinks Khan Giray underdeveloped, but sees Zarema as an improvement on the Circassian heroine of The Prisoner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, and calls the exchange between the two women unusually diverse in feeling, tone and content.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Pushkin</a> himself spoke almost only negatively of it. In October 1823 he wrote to Pyotr Vyazemsky that it was, between them, rubbish, though its epigraph, a quotation from the Persian poet Saadi, was charming; he did think the exchange between Zarema and Maria had dramatic merit.</p><p>The poem inspired many works: Alexander von Zemlinsky's opera Sarema in 1897, Anton Arensky's cantata in 1899, a short film by Yakov Protazanov in 1909 and 1910, Alexander Ilyinsky's opera in 1911, and Boris Asafyev's ballet in 1934. In the 1840s Karl Briullov painted a picture on the subject.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Fountain of Bakhchisaray” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Culturally, the Khan’s Palace at Bakhchysarai, with its Fountain of Tears, inspired Alexander Pushkin’s poem <b>The Fountain of Bakhchisaray</b> (1824), and khans such as Gazi II Giray were noted poets.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
